    <exercise xml:id="ex-cuatroAmigos-count">
      <statement>
        <p>Count: in how many ways could you fill out the table without repeating names?</p>
        <sidebyside widths="55% 40%" margins="0% 0%">
          <stack>
            <p>Each such full table is called a <term>world</term>. Create one example. Then, find all possible worlds!</p>
            <p>Represent all worlds using a <em>tree</em>! (large version in the next page…)</p>
            <image source="cuatroAmigos-tree-empty.png" width="70%">
              <shortdescription></shortdescription>
            </image>
            <p>Total # of worlds: <fillin characters="8"/></p>
            <p><em>How can you be sure that you counted them all?</em></p>
          </stack>
          <tabular>
            <row bottom="minor" header="yes"><cell>Friend</cell><cell>Hobby</cell></row>
            <row><cell></cell><cell>dancing</cell></row>
            <row><cell></cell><cell>sports</cell></row>
            <row><cell></cell><cell>reading</cell></row>
            <row><cell></cell><cell>video games</cell></row>
          </tabular>
        </sidebyside>
        <assemblage component="TODO">
          <title>PENDING: hard-coded cross-reference → xref</title>
          <p>The <q>see the next page…</q> below is a hard-coded layout reference to the tree page. Replace it with an <c>&lt;xref&gt;</c> to <c>ex-cuatroAmigos-tree</c> so it stays correct if page order changes.</p>
        </assemblage>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <image source="cuatroAmigos-tree-full.jpg" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>A four-level tree diagram. From the Start node, the first level branches into 4 (dancing choices), the second into 3 (sports), the third into 2 (reading), and the last into 1 (video games), giving 24 complete branches — one for each world.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      
        <p>Each word corresponds to a complete branch (where we pick a different hobby for each friend). Note that we counted them all, without repeating (why?) So, there are 24 worlds in total (<m>4\times 3\times 2\times 1</m>, or simply <m>4\times 3\times 2</m>).</p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>We could have just not drawn the last branch (why?)</p></li>
          <li><p>Order matters in a branch (why?)</p></li>
          <li><p>Each friend appears exactly once in each branch (why?)</p></li>
          <li><p>We could also do this process not by hobby, but by friend. Do it, and you will see that we get the same result!</p></li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </page>
