  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-cuatroAmigos-compare" workspace="4cm">
      <statement>
        <p>Compare your worlds and answers to check if you all agree with the total possible number of worlds.</p>
        <p>Discuss: Which factors did you use to create your example? Are all worlds equally likely? Do you think that some worlds are impossible or almost impossible? Why?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Students should conclude that there is a total of 24 worlds. Give students time to explore this, instead of guiding them quickly to the answer. Let them pursue different ways to represent the worlds, and ask questions throughout.</p>
          <p>Possible answers in the discussion:</p>
          <p><em>Which factors did you use to create your example?</em>
            <ul>
              <li><p>I looked at the pictures and went for what I thought was most likely. For example, one of the girls should be the reader, and the Asian boy should be playing video games.</p></li>
              <li><p>I thought of some of my friends to represent the fictional kids, and tried to think about their hobbies.</p></li>
              <li><p>I just randomly chose, using a coin.</p></li>
            </ul>
          </p>
          <p><em>Are all universes equally likely?</em>
            <ul>
              <li><p>No! I think that more girls like dancing, for example.</p></li>
              <li><p>Yes! Each has a 1/24 chance of happening, mathematically.</p></li>
              <li><p>I am not too sure…</p></li>
            </ul>
          </p>
          <p><em>Do you think that some universes are impossible or almost impossible? Why?</em>
            <ul>
              <li><p>No. I think all have a decent amount of happening. There's too many people in this world!</p></li>
            </ul>
          </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-cuatroAmigos-puzzle">
      <statement>
        <p>Puzzle time! We are going to use some clues. </p>
          
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Complete these 2 clues with 3 of the kids' names: <alert>You must write each at most once!</alert></p>
            <ul>
              <li><p><fillin characters="12"/> and <fillin characters="12"/> both think that dancing is <alert>not</alert> amusing and sports are <alert>not</alert> fun.</p></li>
              <li><p><fillin characters="12"/>'s hobby is either playing video games or playing sports.</p></li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>How many possible worlds satisfy both clues? (at the same time!) <fillin characters="8"/></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Write a <alert>third</alert> clue to help us narrow down to just 1 solution! (You can now repeat a person's name.)</p>
            <sidebyside widths="30% 68%" margins="0% 0%" valign="middle">
              <p>Use the table for help!</p>
              <tabular>
                <row bottom="medium" header="yes"><cell>Friend</cell><cell>my deductions / possibilities</cell><cell>Hobby is…</cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell></row>
              </tabular>
            </sidebyside>
            
           <sidebyside widths="15% 80%" valign="middle">
              <p>Final clue:</p>
              <p workspace="2cm"></p>
            </sidebyside>

          </li>
        </ol>
        
                    

      </statement>

      <solution>
          <ul>
            <li><p><alert>Hank</alert> and <alert>Leyla</alert> agree in that they don't enjoy dancing nor playing sports.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Alana</alert>'s hobby is either playing video games or playing sports.</p></li>
          </ul>
          <assemblage component="TODO">
            <title>WARNING puzzle-solution names differ from the activity</title>
            <p>EAJ, 5Aug2026</p>
            <p>The solution (TG p.23) uses the names Alana, Hank, Leyla, and Shawn, but the activity's four friends are Axel, Ben, Cora, and Dana. Reconcile the names — the two solution tables below carry them too.</p>
          </assemblage>
          <tabular>
            <row bottom="medium" header="yes">
              <cell>Friend</cell><cell colspan="4">my deductions / possibilities</cell><cell>Hobby is…</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Alana</cell><cell><stale>dan.</stale></cell><cell>spo.</cell><cell><stale>read.</stale></cell><cell>vid.</cell><cell><nbsp/></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Hank</cell><cell><stale>dan.</stale></cell><cell><stale>spo.</stale></cell><cell>read.</cell><cell>vid.</cell><cell><nbsp/></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Leyla</cell><cell><stale>dan.</stale></cell><cell><stale>spo.</stale></cell><cell>read.</cell><cell>vid.</cell><cell><nbsp/></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Shawn</cell><cell>dan.</cell><cell>spo.</cell><cell>read.</cell><cell>vid.</cell><cell>dan.</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>

          <p>Then:</p>

          <tabular>
            <row bottom="medium" header="yes">
              <cell>Friend</cell><cell colspan="4">my deductions / possibilities</cell><cell>Hobby is…</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Alana</cell><cell><stale>dan.</stale></cell><cell>spo.</cell><cell><stale>read.</stale></cell><cell>vid.</cell><cell>spo.</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Hank</cell><cell><stale>dan.</stale></cell><cell><stale>spo.</stale></cell><cell>read.</cell><cell>vid.</cell><cell><nbsp/></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Leyla</cell><cell><stale>dan.</stale></cell><cell><stale>spo.</stale></cell><cell>read.</cell><cell>vid.</cell><cell><nbsp/></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Shawn</cell><cell>dan.</cell><cell><stale>spo.</stale></cell><cell>read.</cell><cell>vid.</cell><cell>dan.</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>

          <p>Solution: 2 worlds satisfy the clues. They are almost equal, just differing in the choices of Hank and Leyla (between reading and playing video games).</p>
          <p>A third clue to narrow down to just one world: There are many options, such as:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Hank plays video games as his hobby.</p></li>
            <li><p>One of the girls plays video games as her hobby.</p></li>
          </ul>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
